328.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-09-30T12:25:28+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-134584386,imgsize-1920909.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Michigan brothers bought 1861 Gilbert Mansion for $1 in 1986; restored property now asks $1.13M]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/134542806.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[In 1986, Christopher and Steve White acquired the Gilbert Mansion in Ypsilanti for just $1. Over 14 months, they transformed the dilapidated historic building into a seven-unit apartment complex. Constructed in 1861, the mansion was on the brink of demolition. Its revival has turned it into a notable landmark in Ypsilanti, and it is currently listed for $1.13 million, exemplifying the power of adaptive reuse.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/real-estate/news/two-michigan-builder-brothers-bought-ypsilantis-crumbling-1861-gilbert-mansion-for-just-1-in-1986-and-spent-14-months-saving-it-from-demolition-four-decades-later-the-restored-property-is-asking-1-13-million/articleshow/134542806.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/real-estate/news/two-michigan-builder-brothers-bought-ypsilantis-crumbling-1861-gilbert-mansion-for-just-1-in-1986-and-spent-14-months-saving-it-from-demolition-four-decades-later-the-restored-property-is-asking-1-13-million/articleshow/134542806.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-09-30T12:04:24+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI Real Estate Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-134542950,imgsize-1969880.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Jordan sank a retired military plane underwater in 2017; it now hosts fish, coral and marine life]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/134582412.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[In 2017, a retired Lockheed C-130 Hercules was submerged off the coast of Aqaba to establish an artificial reef, promoting marine biodiversity and dive tourism. Since its descent, various marine species have thrived among the wreck, offering refuge for fish. The aircraft's distinctive allure has drawn divers, enhancing the region's underwater experiences. Despite enduring storm damage in 2020, it remains a vibrant habitat and popular dive location.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/nature/in-2017-jordan-sank-a-retired-30-metre-c-130-hercules-off-aqaba-to-create-an-artificial-reef-the-aircraft-now-rests-about-16-metres-underwater-and-attracts-coral-fish-and-other-marine-life/articleshow/134582412.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/nature/in-2017-jordan-sank-a-retired-30-metre-c-130-hercules-off-aqaba-to-create-an-artificial-reef-the-aircraft-now-rests-about-16-metres-underwater-and-attracts-coral-fish-and-other-marine-life/articleshow/134582412.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-09-30T12:00:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI Science Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-134582457,imgsize-642369.cms"/></item></channel></rss>